---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../../components/FurtherReading.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import { celestial, ocean } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Saturn Return', href: '/explore/saturn-return/' },
];
const returnTimeline = [
  ['Birth', 'Natal Saturn sets the degree, sign, house, aspects, and condition that the return will revisit.'],
  ['~29–30', 'The first Saturn return.'],
  ['~58–59', 'The second Saturn return.'],
  ['~88–89', 'A third return is possible later in life.'],
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Saturn Return. What it is and when yours arrives"
  description="Saturn takes about 29.5 years to circle the zodiac, returning to its natal position around ages 29 and 58. Learn what astrologers look for without turning the transit into a crisis script."
  path="/explore/saturn-return/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>The Saturn return</h1>
        <p class="lede">Saturn takes about 29.5 years to come back to its natal position, which gives this transit its famous timing around the end of the twenties and again near the end of the fifties. The cycle is real. The internet's crisis script is not required.</p>
      </div>
      <img class="hero__img" src={ocean.solitaryBuoy.src} srcset={ocean.solitaryBuoy.srcset} sizes="100vw" alt={ocean.solitaryBuoy.alt} width={ocean.solitaryBuoy.width} height={ocean.solitaryBuoy.height} style="transform: scaleX(-1);" fetchpriority="high" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Age tells you when to look. Your Saturn tells you what you're looking at.</h2>
      <div class="return-timeline" aria-label="Saturn return timeline">
        {returnTimeline.map(([age, note]) => (
          <article>
            <strong>{age}</strong>
            <p>{note}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <p style="margin: 0;"><strong>A return is a period, not one birthday.</strong> Saturn can cross its natal degree more than once because of retrograde motion, so the exact passes may unfold across months.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>The mechanics, first</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/explore/planets/saturn/">Saturn</a> takes approximately 29.5 years to travel the
        zodiac. When transiting Saturn returns to the sign and degree it occupied at birth, you are
        in a Saturn return. The first is usually around ages 29–30, the second around 58–59, with a
        third possible much later in life.
      </p>
      <p>
        Saturn can cross the natal degree more than once because of retrograde motion, so a return
        is better understood as a period than as one dramatic day.
      </p>

      <h2>What astrologers look for</h2>
      <p>
        Saturn is associated with structure, responsibility, limits, time, commitment, and earned
        authority. During a return, those themes come back into direct relationship with the natal
        Saturn. The sign, house, aspects, and condition of Saturn tell us far more than age alone.
      </p>
      <p>
        For one person the return may coincide with professional responsibility becoming heavier;
        for another it may concentrate on partnership, home, health routines, money, study, or a
        quieter internal restructuring. The transit does not come with a universal checklist.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.kintsugi} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>The part worth taking seriously</h2>
      <p>
        Saturn returns can feel consequential because they often coincide with questions about what
        is sustainable, what has become too small, what requires commitment, and which structures no
        longer deserve to be maintained. That does not automatically mean loss or upheaval. A sound
        structure can deepen just as readily as an unsound one can become harder to ignore.
      </p>
      <p>
        I am less interested in telling someone what a Saturn return is “supposed” to do than in
        looking at the actual Saturn in that chart and asking where the pressure, responsibility,
        consolidation, or maturation is showing up in the life being lived.
      </p>

      <h2>Questions worth bringing to the cycle</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>What has become more consequential than it used to feel?</li>
        <li>Where am I being asked to build more carefully rather than more quickly?</li>
        <li>Which commitments are genuinely mine, and which have simply accumulated?</li>
        <li>What am I ready to take responsibility for that I was not ready to carry before?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>When is mine?</h2>
      <p>
        Your birth date gives Saturn's natal sign and degree; a reliable birth time adds the house
        and therefore important context about where the return is operating. If you are near the
        usual age window, the exact passes and the rest of the chart matter more than the number on
        your birthday.
      </p>
      <p>
        Related: <a href="/explore/transits/">how transits work</a> and
        <a href="/explore/planets/saturn/">Saturn in the chart</a>.
      </p>
      <FurtherReading />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Is the Saturn return a real question in your life now?"
    body="The Reading Finder can separate a first reading from follow-up timing work and point you toward the conversation that fits where you actually are."
    buttonLabel="Use the Reading Finder"
    buttonHref="/reading-finder/"
    secondaryLabel="Keep learning about transits"
    secondaryHref="/explore/transits/"
  />

  <style>
    .return-timeline {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-4);
      margin-block: var(--space-6);
    }
    .return-timeline article {
      padding: var(--space-5);
      border-top: 3px solid currentColor;
      border-radius: 0 0 var(--radius-lg, 1rem) var(--radius-lg, 1rem);
    }
    .return-timeline strong { display: block; font-size: var(--text-xl); margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
    .return-timeline p { margin: 0; }
    @media (min-width: 768px) {
      .return-timeline { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
